# ── CLOUDFLARE ORIGIN CA — RSA ROOT ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The root that signed `gateway/certs/origin.crt`, and the reason this directory
# exists at all.
#
#   subject/issuer  C=US, O=CloudFlare, Inc., OU=CloudFlare Origin SSL
#                   Certificate Authority, L=San Francisco, ST=California
#   sha256          D3:C7:E8:5C:91:70:7F:C0:A1:2A:BC:5D:88:26:67:47:
#                   AA:4F:A8:E7:B1:62:F6:33:FF:B3:C9:D9:89:94:76:20
#   not after       2029-08-15
#   source          https://developers.cloudflare.com/ssl/static/origin_ca_rsa_root.pem
#
# ── WHY IT IS COMMITTED RATHER THAN DOWNLOADED ────────────────────────────────
#
# `scripts/gateway-cert.sh` builds the trust bundle from this file, and that
# script runs inside `scripts/estate-up.sh` — unattended, on a box whose whole
# job is to come back up. A `curl` there makes bringing the estate up depend on
# reaching developers.cloudflare.com, and the failure would land as a bundle
# missing one root, i.e. as every internal check failing against the mainnet
# gateway with nothing saying why. This is a PUBLIC root certificate; there is
# nothing here to protect and nothing to keep fresh but an expiry date, which is
# written above so the renewal is a date rather than a surprise.
#
# ── AND WHY IT IS NOT IN THE SYSTEM TRUST STORE, HERE OR ANYWHERE ─────────────
#
# Cloudflare does not ship this root to browsers or operating systems, and it
# should not be added to one. It signs ORIGIN certificates — the leg between
# Cloudflare's edge and a customer's origin server — and trusting it in a machine
# trust store would make every one of those certificates, for every Cloudflare
# customer, valid to that machine for the names it carries. The estate's own CA
# has the same property and the same rule, which is why `gateway-cert.sh` prints
# the trust command for `ca.crt` and does not run it.
#
# So this root is trusted NARROWLY: `gateway/certs/trust.crt` is the file passed
# to `curl --cacert` and `NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS`, one process at a time, and never
# installed anywhere. That is a deliberate difference from appending it to
# `ca.crt`, which is the file the owner HAS installed in a keychain.
#
# ── ANY .pem IN THIS DIRECTORY JOINS THE BUNDLE ───────────────────────────────
#
# `gateway-cert.sh` concatenates `ca.crt` with every `gateway/trust/*.pem`, so a
# second edge provider is one file and no code. The bundle is rebuilt from these
# inputs on every run rather than appended to, which is what makes it idempotent.
